<p><em>Think about the last time something broke at 3am.</em></p>

<p>How long did it take to find the person who could actually fix it? Not the
person who got paged. The person who could fix it.</p>

<p>That gap is not a communication problem, and it is not a tooling problem.
It comes from two decisions most organizations never actually made. What
matters most, and who is accountable for keeping it up. A service catalog
does not make either decision. It only records the ones you already made,
which is why catalog rollouts stall out with metadata nobody trusts.</p>

<p>This diagnostic scores where you stand on both, across five dimensions:
inventory, ownership, criticality, alignment, and enforcement. Twenty
questions, about ten minutes. You get a score out of 100, a breakdown
showing which dimension is dragging, and one thing to fix first.</p>

<p>It is a spreadsheet, so you can fill it in with your team and argue about
the answers. The arguments are the useful part.</p>

